<section id="mistakes">
<h2>Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

<p>Using AI for SEO content is not hard. Using it well — producing content that ranks and retains readers — is where most people go wrong. Here are the mistakes we see most often.</p>

<h3>Mistake 1: Publishing Without Editing</h3>
<p>AI output reads like AI output. It uses predictable structures. It repeats itself. It hedges. Readers spot it immediately, and Google's algorithms are catching up. Always run at least the three editing passes described above. If you do nothing else, do this.</p>

<h3>Mistake 2: Trusting AI-Generated Facts</h3>
<p>AI writing tools do not "know" things. They generate statistically probable sequences of words. That means they sometimes produce entirely fictional statistics, cite non-existent studies, or describe product features that the product does not have. Verify everything before publishing.</p>

<h3>Mistake 3: Skipping the Outline Review</h3>
<p>The AI generates an outline based on what top-ranking pages do. But those pages might be outdated, thin, or missing the actual search intent. If you skip the outline review, you commit to producing a slightly better version of the same mediocre content everyone else published. Review the outline. Make it better than the top results — not just a copy of them.</p>

<h3>Mistake 4: Ignoring EEAT Signals</h3>
<p>Google's quality guidelines — Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, Trustworthiness — apply to every piece of content you publish. AI-generated content often scores zero on all four. Fix this by: adding author bios, citing primary sources, including real data and examples, and linking to your own original research. These signals tell Google the content is not auto-generated filler.</p>

<h3>Mistake 5: Using AI for Everything</h3>
<p>AI writes the draft. You write the headlines, the meta description, the introduction hook, the conclusion, and the call to action. These are the elements readers see first, and they determine whether anyone reads the rest. Keep the human voice where it matters most.</p>
</section>

<section id="editing">
<h2>Editing AI-Generated Content for Humans</h2>

<p>If someone reads 200 words of your article and cannot tell whether a human or an AI wrote it, they assume AI — and they leave. Here are the editing practices that make AI-assisted content feel human-written.</p>

<p><strong>Vary sentence rhythm.</strong> AI writing tends toward uniform sentence length: medium, medium, medium. Humans vary. Mix short, punchy sentences with longer ones. Break up dense paragraphs with single-sentence lines when the point is important enough to stand alone.</p>

<p><strong>Remove hedging language.</strong> AI-generated content overuses qualifiers: "it is important to note," "many experts believe," "it can be argued that." These add word count without adding value. Delete them. Replace with direct statements.</p>

<p><strong>Add specific examples.</strong> AI writing is abstract. It says "optimise your content for search engines." A human says "add the keyword in your first 100 words, include it in at least one H2, and use 3–5 related terms the top-ranking pages also use." Specificity signals expertise. Add it everywhere the AI draft is vague.</p>

<p><strong>Cut the fluff.</strong> AI-generated articles are often 20–30% longer than they need to be. The extra length is padding — repeated points, circular explanations, unnecessary background. The edit that most improves AI content is deletion. Cut mercilessly.</p>

<p><strong>Add an original data point, anecdote, or insight.</strong> This is the single highest-impact edit. AI cannot generate your experience. If you have tested a tool, share what happened. If you have data from your own site, include it. One original contribution per article signals to readers — and to Google — that the content was not auto-generated.</p>
</section>

