<section id="optimisation">
<h2>Post-Writing SEO Optimisation</h2>

<p>Once the draft is written and edited, the optimisation pass makes it search-competitive. This step is mechanical — it does not require creativity, just thoroughness.</p>

<p><strong>On-page checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Title tag</strong> — Include the primary keyword. Keep it under 60 characters for display. Make it clickable, not robotic.</li>
<li><strong>Meta description</strong> — 150–160 characters. Include the keyword once. Tell the searcher what they will learn. End with a reason to click.</li>
<li><strong>H1</strong> — Include the primary keyword. Should differ from the title tag.</li>
<li><strong>H2/H3 structure</strong> — Logical hierarchy. No skipped levels. At least 2–3 H2s should contain the target keyword or close variants.</li>
<li><strong>First 100 words</strong> — Include the primary keyword naturally.</li>
<li><strong>Image alt text</strong> — Descriptive, keyword-relevant where natural, not stuffed.</li>
<li><strong>Internal links</strong> — 3–5 contextual links to your own related content.</li>
<li><strong>External links</strong> — 1–3 links to authoritative sources that support your claims.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Optimisation tools that help:</strong> SEO-specific AI writers handle most of this during generation. If you are using a general AI assistant, pair it with Surfer SEO or Semrush's Content Editor for the optimisation pass. Both score your draft against real SERP data and tell you exactly what to fix. For a deeper look, read our reviews of <a href="/reviews/seo-writing-ai-review/">SEO Writing AI</a> and <a href="/reviews/surfer-seo-review/">Surfer SEO</a>.</p>

<p><strong>After publishing:</strong> Monitor the article's ranking weekly for the first month. If it does not break the top 30, revisit: check keyword difficulty, improve internal linking from higher-authority pages, or expand sections the top-ranking pages cover more thoroughly.</p>
</section>

