<section id="faq">
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<FaqItem question="Can Google detect AI-written content?">
<p>Google does not penalise AI-generated content automatically. Its guidelines state that content should be "helpful, reliable, and people-first" regardless of how it was produced. However, unedited AI content often fails EEAT quality signals — shallow structure, generic language, and lack of original insight — which indirectly hurts rankings. Human-edited AI content that meets the same quality standards as human-written content can rank well.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="What is the best AI tool for SEO content writing?">
<p>The best tool depends on your volume and budget. Purpose-built tools like SEO Writing AI generate search-optimised drafts from a single keyword, including SERP analysis and NLP scoring. They are efficient for high-volume publishers. General AI assistants like ChatGPT and Claude offer more flexibility but require more manual structuring and editing. For most affiliate marketers and bloggers, a purpose-built SEO AI writer saves 2–3 hours per article compared to a general assistant.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="How do I edit AI content to sound human?">
<p>Three passes: structure (fix flow, delete fluff), voice (rewrite generic phrasing, add specific examples, vary sentence rhythm), and accuracy (verify every claim). The single highest-impact edit is removing hedging language — "it is important to note," "many experts suggest," "it could be argued" — and replacing it with direct, confident statements. Also add at least one original data point or anecdote per article.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="Can I rank on Google using only AI-written content?">
<p>Yes, if you edit thoroughly. Unedited AI content rarely ranks for competitive keywords because it fails to differentiate from the top-ranking pages it was trained on. The content that ranks is the content that adds something the SERP does not already have — a unique perspective, original data, a better structure, or a clearer explanation. AI can accelerate the drafting. You provide the differentiation.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="How long should an AI-assisted SEO article be?">
<p>Match or slightly exceed the average word count of the top 10 ranking pages for your target keyword. This is almost always more reliable than a fixed word-count rule. In practice, most competitive informational queries in 2026 produce top-ranking pages in the 1,500–2,500 word range. Product reviews and comparisons tend toward the higher end. How-to guides vary widely. Let the SERP data — not an arbitrary target — determine your length.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="Do I need an SEO optimisation tool if I use an AI writer?">
<p>Purpose-built SEO AI writers include basic optimisation (keyword usage, heading structure, meta tags). But they do not typically score your draft against live SERP data the way dedicated tools like Surfer SEO do. For competitive keywords, running your edited draft through an optimisation tool is the difference between "good enough" and "optimised to compete." For low-competition keywords, the AI writer's built-in optimisation may be sufficient.</p>
</FaqItem>

<FaqItem question="Is AI content considered duplicate content?">
<p>No. AI-generated text is original in the technical sense — it is not copied from a specific source. Google's duplicate content policy applies to identical or substantially similar blocks of content across URLs. Two different AI-generated articles on the same topic will produce different text. However, unedited AI content from the same tool often shares structural similarities that make it read as templated, which can reduce perceived quality even if it passes duplicate checks.</p>
</FaqItem>
